00:32:06.040 --> 00:32:10.780
But it was literally the day before we got you guys on a plane to go to New York.

782
00:32:11.320 --> 00:32:12.420
Well, actually.

783
00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:12.980
Did we go?

784
00:32:13.140 --> 00:32:13.900
I don't even remember.

785
00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:18.200
We got ourselves on a plane because NBC said they wouldn't pay for it.

786
00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:19.200
Yeah, that's right.

787
00:32:19.380 --> 00:32:24.360
So the four of us were all, like, self-traveling.

788
00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:24.620
Yeah.

789
00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:27.840
And then, I don't know who, Terry, maybe it was you.

790
00:32:27.980 --> 00:32:30.940
But after we got back, someone made a stink and said,

791
00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:35.440
I can't believe you made those actors pay their own way to the NBC Upfront's announcement.

792
00:32:35.560 --> 00:32:37.160
And we all got reimbursed.

793
00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:39.340
Oh, someone's raising her hand.

794
00:32:39.880 --> 00:32:41.520
Yeah, that was infuriating.

795
00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:44.820
Yeah, because they didn't pay for any of us.

796
00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:45.420
You found that out.

797
00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:47.080
Brian and I flew ourselves out, too.

798
00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:47.740
Yeah.

799
00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:51.060
After that year, NBC would fly us out.

800
00:32:51.400 --> 00:32:51.660
Yeah.

801
00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:53.280
But that's all you.

802
00:32:53.380 --> 00:32:56.040
God, Jenna, I just thought about that one after party.

803
00:32:56.260 --> 00:32:58.380
Where I broke my back at Foodicon.

804
00:32:58.720 --> 00:32:59.020
Yes.

805
00:32:59.200 --> 00:32:59.400
Yeah.

806
00:32:59.740 --> 00:32:59.980
Yeah.

807
00:33:00.260 --> 00:33:00.500
Oh, my God.

808
00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:01.820
That was insane.

809
00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:03.620
That was insane.

810
00:33:03.940 --> 00:33:04.180
Mm-hmm.

811
00:33:04.340 --> 00:33:08.580
Can I tell a story, though, that I think NBC was, like, really worried I was going to sue

812
00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:09.820
because it was their party?

813
00:33:10.200 --> 00:33:10.820
Of course they were.

814
00:33:10.900 --> 00:33:11.160
Right?

815
00:33:11.320 --> 00:33:12.380
And I didn't.

816
00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:14.400
I didn't even—I don't know.

817
00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:19.240
They put me up at the Ritz-Carlton because that's where they had put me, even though I'd

818
00:33:19.240 --> 00:33:20.120
asked to stay in Soho.

819
00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:21.920
I just want to say I requested a cheaper hotel.

820
00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:23.900
But they're like, no, everybody has to stay at this one hotel.

821
00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:24.760
We need to keep you together.

822
00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:25.640
You couldn't physically move.

823
00:33:25.740 --> 00:33:27.140
But I couldn't physically move for two weeks.

824
00:33:27.260 --> 00:33:29.020
So they paid my hotel bill.

825
00:33:29.060 --> 00:33:29.320
Yes.

826
00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:30.640
As I convalesced.

827
00:33:30.900 --> 00:33:31.160
And mine.

828
00:33:31.320 --> 00:33:32.160
And Angela's.

829
00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:33.300
So she could be with me.

830
00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:38.520
And then I, like, slowly recovered and I got ready to start the new season.

831
00:33:38.780 --> 00:33:40.700
And Ben Silverman took me out.

832
00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:42.700
This could be on Office Leeds Burn It to the Ground.

833
00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:46.040
It's not totally horrible or anything, but it's just, you know, a story.

834
00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:52.180
He took me out to dinner to sushi because he said, hey, I just want to make sure you're

835
00:33:52.180 --> 00:33:52.840
feeling okay.

836
00:33:53.060 --> 00:33:54.500
You're up for the new season.

837
00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:58.960
And we got you this gift to say that we're just so sorry for the hardship.

838
00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:01.000
And it was this beautiful watch.

839
00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:02.320
It was beautiful.

840
00:34:02.460 --> 00:34:04.600
I was like, oh, my God, how expensive is this watch?

841
00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:05.880
This watch must be so expensive.

842
00:34:06.100 --> 00:34:07.440
I keep it in a safe.

843
00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:09.020
All these years I've coveted it.

844
00:34:09.180 --> 00:34:09.660
Oh, my God.

845
00:34:09.760 --> 00:34:10.760
Was it an Oceanaught?

846
00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:11.620
An Oceanaught!

847
00:34:11.840 --> 00:34:14.970
It was an Oceanaught!

848
00:34:14.970 --> 00:34:15.390
Are you kidding me?

849
00:34:15.530 --> 00:34:16.690
No, I am not kidding you.

850
00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:20.510
And I Googled it, and it's worth, like, a hundred bucks.

851
00:34:20.990 --> 00:34:23.890
I thought it was worth thousands, thousands of dollars.

852
00:34:24.370 --> 00:34:26.290
I was trying to get it insured.

853
00:34:26.810 --> 00:34:28.870
Because I thought it's time.

854
00:34:28.890 --> 00:34:30.790
I should have done this years ago.

855
00:34:30.970 --> 00:34:32.010
Do you have one, too?

856
00:34:32.190 --> 00:34:33.110
I have one, too.

857
00:34:33.290 --> 00:34:37.270
And I actually think that Oceanaught may have sent them to us.

858
00:34:37.270 --> 00:34:38.350
So he didn't even pay for it.

859
00:34:38.350 --> 00:34:43.430
So they were just in a closet at Reveille, and he just grabbed it, and he didn't even buy

860
00:34:43.430 --> 00:34:44.050
it for me.

861
00:34:44.470 --> 00:34:45.470
Ben Silverman!

862
00:34:45.710 --> 00:34:47.710
Ben Silverman, you cheap bastard!

863
00:34:49.030 --> 00:34:50.330
How dare you!

864
00:34:50.990 --> 00:34:53.790
You owe me a luxury watch, Ben Silverman!

865
00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:54.610
Ben's been keeping it in a safe!

866
00:34:54.610 --> 00:34:54.870
He does.

867
00:34:55.210 --> 00:34:56.930
Ben's been keeping it in a safe!

868
00:34:56.990 --> 00:34:58.630
You owe Jenna a luxury watch.

869
00:34:59.350 --> 00:35:01.710
I mean, we love you, Oceanaught, but you know.

870
00:35:01.770 --> 00:35:01.970
Yeah.

871
00:35:02.190 --> 00:35:05.170
She's been keeping the Oceanaught in a safe for a decade.

872
00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:08.090
I've been keeping my $70 watch in a safe for 15 years.

873
00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:09.670
I think you might want to pull it out.

874
00:35:09.670 --> 00:35:10.850
Maybe give it some wear.

875
00:35:11.470 --> 00:35:12.290
I wear it.

876
00:35:12.410 --> 00:35:13.050
I wear it.

877
00:35:13.130 --> 00:35:16.450
But honestly, like, sometimes I don't wear it because I'm like, I don't want someone

878
00:35:16.450 --> 00:35:17.210
to mug me.

879
00:35:17.210 --> 00:35:18.330
Nobody's mugging you, baby.

880
00:35:18.650 --> 00:35:19.110
No, no.

881
00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:21.990
Amazing.

882
00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:24.650
That is so funny, Jenna.

883
00:35:24.830 --> 00:35:25.430
That is so amazing.

884
00:35:25.430 --> 00:35:26.630
I'm dying about that.

885
00:35:26.630 --> 00:35:27.930
That is amazing, actually.

886
00:35:28.410 --> 00:35:33.810
Well, Terri, one of the things about you as a producer was that I felt really seen as

887
00:35:33.810 --> 00:35:35.610
both an artist and a human.

888
00:35:35.850 --> 00:35:41.310
That was a unique thing that you brought to your producing, and I know that it's something

889
00:35:41.310 --> 00:35:43.530
that you continue to do.

890
00:35:44.130 --> 00:35:49.390
And I want to pivot for a second because I want to talk about some of the ways that you

891
00:35:49.390 --> 00:35:54.530
really work to encourage other women, especially in this business.

892
00:35:54.770 --> 00:35:54.910
Yeah.

893
00:35:55.070 --> 00:35:56.810
I was looking to get into producing.

894
00:35:57.030 --> 00:35:58.390
I reached out to you.

895
00:35:58.550 --> 00:36:02.070
I had this script, and I said, how do I develop this?

896
00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:03.470
How do I get this off the ground?

897
00:36:03.590 --> 00:36:04.510
You mentored me.

898
00:36:04.930 --> 00:36:05.030
Yeah.

899
00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:09.370
Could you talk to the people out there who might be listening, who might be interested

900
00:36:09.370 --> 00:36:12.730
in wanting to work as a storyteller in the entertainment industry?

901
00:36:12.730 --> 00:36:14.290
Audrey, what advice do you have?

902
00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:16.550
Because I know you have some that will be very valuable.

903
00:36:16.910 --> 00:36:17.090
Yeah.

904
00:36:17.270 --> 00:36:21.730
I mean, I have a lot of these little pearls that I can drop, but one thing that I always

905
00:36:21.730 --> 00:36:23.830
say is that what we do is really hard.

906
00:36:24.230 --> 00:36:27.010
It's a hard industry to be in, and so you better love what you do.

907
00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:31.830
You have to start with something that you're passionate about because you can smell when somebody

908
00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:34.470
is just trying to do something that they don't really care about.

909
00:36:35.170 --> 00:36:36.510
And I can feel that.

910
00:36:36.510 --> 00:36:40.510
And you can also feel that at the end of the day if and when that work gets made.

911
00:36:40.970 --> 00:36:44.390
And so I think it's an insane amount of tenacity.

912
00:36:44.710 --> 00:36:46.790
I mean, my job is to get a yes.

913
00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:51.030
And until I get that yes, I'm going to do whatever I have to do to get that yes.

914
00:36:51.390 --> 00:36:56.370
And to use the experience and the knowledge that I have, but also you have to be willing

915
00:36:56.370 --> 00:37:03.130
to put yourself out there and annoy the shit out of people so that they see you in front

916
00:37:03.130 --> 00:37:08.290
of them, you know, because we're in a big business and people remember sort of what was

917
00:37:08.290 --> 00:37:10.470
in front of them in the last moment.

918
00:37:10.610 --> 00:37:14.570
And if Terry keeps calling and trying to get a piece of talent or try to sell something,

919
00:37:15.230 --> 00:37:18.550
you know, I always say, if I call one more time, they're going to put me in jail, have

920
00:37:18.550 --> 00:37:24.650
me arrested, but I end up getting my show made, you know, so I think it's tenacity, an

921
00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:28.870
amazing amount of discipline because it takes a long time sometimes to get things made.

922
00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:33.590
And, you know, I've had things in development for five, six, seven years sometimes.

923
00:37:34.070 --> 00:37:41.550
And produce what you love and always go back to the place of why am I telling this story?

924
00:37:41.930 --> 00:37:43.650
Why am I telling it now?

925
00:37:44.330 --> 00:37:47.630
And who's going to care or what life is it going to affect?

926
00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:53.870
So for me, it's really important that I know I'm putting something out in the world that

927
00:37:53.870 --> 00:37:56.190
can maybe affect one person's life.

928
00:37:56.190 --> 00:38:01.430
Make them laugh, make them cry, make them see a community of people that you don't normally

929
00:38:01.430 --> 00:38:04.350
get to see, relationships that aren't obvious.

930
00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:06.030
And that's the work.

931
00:38:06.170 --> 00:38:11.910
That's the joy for me is using the platform that I have to get stories out there that might

932
00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:15.510
not have had that opportunity to be produced.

933
00:38:15.950 --> 00:38:18.550
That's very much what you told me at our lunch.

934
00:38:18.650 --> 00:38:18.850
Yeah.

935
00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:19.810
Right there.

936
00:38:20.030 --> 00:38:21.310
Yeah, it's my heart, my guts.

937
00:38:21.570 --> 00:38:21.930
Yeah.

938
00:38:21.930 --> 00:38:27.930
And then you have to have the courage to just count on both of those things and not let

939
00:38:27.930 --> 00:38:29.470
anybody push that around.

940
00:38:29.830 --> 00:38:35.010
And you have to be willing to say no when somebody says, I want to buy your show, but I want

941
00:38:35.010 --> 00:38:37.950
to, I know you're selling a drama, but we really want it to be a half hour comedy.

942
00:38:38.190 --> 00:38:41.770
You have to be willing to say, no, I'm not willing to do that just to have a show on the

943
00:38:41.770 --> 00:38:41.950
air.

944
00:38:42.170 --> 00:38:42.530
Right.

945
00:38:42.910 --> 00:38:43.110
Yeah.

946
00:38:43.110 --> 00:38:49.390
You know, so knowing and creating the boundaries of protecting the work and having the courage

947
00:38:49.390 --> 00:38:53.010
to say no, if it doesn't feel right, instead of just saying yes, because you're getting

948
00:38:53.010 --> 00:38:53.410
a sale.

949
00:38:54.510 --> 00:38:57.270
I should say I did end up selling that show.

950
00:38:57.390 --> 00:38:57.850
You did?

951
00:38:58.030 --> 00:38:58.830
To Warner Brothers.

952
00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:00.190
It just didn't go anywhere.

953
00:39:00.510 --> 00:39:01.890
But I did get the sale.

954
00:39:01.890 --> 00:39:02.190
That's a big deal.

955
00:39:02.290 --> 00:39:03.170
And I was excited.

956
00:39:03.530 --> 00:39:05.090
That's a big deal.

957
00:39:05.230 --> 00:39:05.390
Yeah.

958
00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:11.110
And I sold a comedy as well that again, they didn't end up getting made, but I felt like I

959
00:39:11.610 --> 00:39:12.450
You got it done.

960
00:39:12.590 --> 00:39:13.370
I got it done.

961
00:39:13.450 --> 00:39:14.670
And I'm proud of that.

962
00:39:14.790 --> 00:39:18.450
And that was very much due to your coaching throughout that process.

963
00:39:18.650 --> 00:39:19.490
But also you did it.

964
00:39:19.790 --> 00:39:20.150
I did it.

965
00:39:20.150 --> 00:39:23.030
You used the tools that you learned and you got it done.

966
00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:24.470
And now you have that experience.

967
00:39:24.710 --> 00:39:28.510
So the next time you do it, you know what you need to do to get that thing made.

968
00:39:28.750 --> 00:39:29.010
Yeah.

969
00:39:29.190 --> 00:39:31.790
I didn't get paid for it though, when I sold it.

970
00:39:31.830 --> 00:39:32.030
Join the club.

971
00:39:32.250 --> 00:39:32.750
I know.

972
00:39:32.910 --> 00:39:34.550
That's something that I think people don't know.

973
00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:37.650
Until it gets made, my writer got paid.

974
00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:41.970
You know, the showrunner I had attached, they got paid to write the pilot.

975
00:39:42.390 --> 00:39:48.910
People do not understand how unsung non-writing producers are because you can develop something

976
00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:51.290
for years and not get paid for it until it sells.

977
00:39:51.510 --> 00:39:51.710
Yeah.

978
00:39:51.950 --> 00:39:54.330
And most people can't afford to do that.

979
00:39:54.630 --> 00:39:55.050
Yeah.

980
00:39:55.130 --> 00:39:56.690
Which is why there's not a lot of us.

981
00:39:56.690 --> 00:40:04.910
Um, but because I'm at a place in my career and I've gone to studios and said, I need

982
00:40:04.910 --> 00:40:06.290
to get paid to develop this.

983
00:40:06.470 --> 00:40:10.370
I should get paid to develop it because it's going to get better because of what I'm going

984
00:40:10.370 --> 00:40:11.330
to bring to the project.

985
00:40:11.830 --> 00:40:14.470
Especially if a studio comes to me and says, we have this great project.

986
00:40:14.570 --> 00:40:15.990
Will you come produce it?

987
00:40:16.670 --> 00:40:19.830
If I love it and I align with it, yes.

988
00:40:20.090 --> 00:40:21.450
If you pay me, yes.

989
00:40:22.550 --> 00:40:22.950
Yeah.

990
00:40:22.970 --> 00:40:26.770
You know, so it's really hard to make a living as a, as a producer.

991
00:40:27.290 --> 00:40:29.870
I sold two shows, but I wrote them.

992
00:40:30.090 --> 00:40:31.630
I pitched it and I wrote them.

993
00:40:32.270 --> 00:40:34.210
I wrote the pilot, so I did get paid.

994
00:40:34.550 --> 00:40:36.470
Um, and neither of them got made.

995
00:40:36.610 --> 00:40:43.290
One came sort of close, but I think what I needed was after I wrote the pilot and got

996
00:40:43.290 --> 00:40:46.590
paid for the pilot, I needed someone to help me get it to that next place.

997
00:40:46.730 --> 00:40:52.770
Cause what I didn't understand really was sort of the politics of how then your pilot

998
00:40:52.770 --> 00:40:57.330
goes from being bought, written, goes through all the network notes.

999
00:40:57.510 --> 00:41:06.190
And then the people that bought my show then left the network and my show just got shelved.

1000
00:41:06.430 --> 00:41:10.190
So it's so wonderful, Terry, that you just show up for people.

1001
00:41:10.190 --> 00:41:14.590
And over the years, Jenna and I have both gotten texts from you, like here, if you ever

1002
00:41:14.590 --> 00:41:17.670
need anything and you mean it, that's the other thing.

1003
00:41:17.750 --> 00:41:21.270
It's like how you read Jenna's script and immediately got back to her.

1004
00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:23.130
It's something I really admire about you.

1005
00:41:23.250 --> 00:41:24.070
You're not talk.

1006
00:41:24.210 --> 00:41:26.230
You, if you say you're going to do something, you do it.

1007
00:41:26.510 --> 00:41:32.270
And I think that was a great thing that we saw you do all the time on the show, um, that

1008
00:41:32.270 --> 00:41:33.370
I definitely took with me.

1009
00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:35.970
You know, integrity is really important to me.

1010
00:41:36.830 --> 00:41:40.410
Um, I, I, I mean, first of all, I love you guys.

1011
00:41:40.530 --> 00:41:45.230
I'll do anything for you, but I think it's really important.

1012
00:41:45.490 --> 00:41:49.930
I need to wake up with myself in the morning and know that I've done good things and I've

1013
00:41:49.930 --> 00:41:53.490
tried to help people and I don't have an ego about, I think there's room for all kinds

1014
00:41:53.490 --> 00:41:56.150
of people to do what we do, especially women.

1015
00:41:56.150 --> 00:41:59.430
And if we can help each other, why wouldn't we?

1016
00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:02.410
I'm not competitive with other women.

1017
00:42:02.590 --> 00:42:04.510
I want, there's room for all of us.

1018
00:42:04.510 --> 00:42:09.970
So I think it's really important that I become that vessel for some people to be able to help

1019
00:42:09.970 --> 00:42:14.770
them so that we can continue on with generations of women doing the work.

1020
00:42:14.930 --> 00:42:16.390
And you're so doing that.

1021
00:42:16.550 --> 00:42:18.030
You're a keynote speaker now.

1022
00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:20.750
You did a TED Talk, which was amazing.

1023
00:42:20.850 --> 00:42:22.050
Jenna and I both watched it.

1024
00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:26.030
It's titled Aging into Power, which I appreciated that first word.

1025
00:42:28.250 --> 00:42:30.670
Can you tell us about that and how that came about?

1026
00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:41.170
Yes, there was a gentleman who was putting together this series called Bold and Provocative Ideas

1027
00:42:41.170 --> 00:42:42.350
That Can Save the World.

1028
00:42:42.570 --> 00:42:45.090
That was the theme behind this particular TED Talk.

1029
00:42:45.550 --> 00:42:51.230
And he called me up and said, I came to one of your keynotes or I was on a panel or something.

1030
00:42:51.350 --> 00:42:53.010
Have you ever thought about doing a TED Talk?

1031
00:42:54.430 --> 00:42:58.010
And without even thinking about it, I said, yes.

1032
00:42:58.870 --> 00:43:00.450
I never thought about doing a TED Talk.

1033
00:43:00.710 --> 00:43:01.950
I just said, yes.

1034
00:43:02.630 --> 00:43:07.570
And he said, I'm doing a series called Bold and Provocative Ideas That Can Change the World.

1035
00:43:08.070 --> 00:43:16.790
And I said, I'm not sure what my bold and provocative idea is, but I'll figure it out because I'm going to do this.

1036
00:43:17.870 --> 00:43:26.030
And I don't know who was talking at that time because it was one of the scariest things I've ever done in my life.

1037
00:43:26.210 --> 00:43:33.790
I mean, I've stood in front of theaters of people and talked about shows and everything that I do behind the camera.

1038
00:43:34.030 --> 00:43:36.650
I don't talk about my life very much.

1039
00:43:38.210 --> 00:43:45.850
And I'm pretty sacred about my personal life and things that I have gone through as an executive and as a human.

1040
00:43:46.050 --> 00:43:55.710
And I thought, maybe it's time for me to step out and share my story so that maybe my story, now my personal story, will help other women.

1041
00:43:55.830 --> 00:43:59.970
And what I hear so much is, I don't know.

1042
00:44:00.070 --> 00:44:01.150
I'm not good enough.

1043
00:44:01.150 --> 00:44:01.750
I don't know.

1044
00:44:01.870 --> 00:44:02.890
I don't have the experience.

1045
00:44:02.950 --> 00:44:03.770
I don't know.

1046
00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:05.370
I think I'm too old to do that.

1047
00:44:05.370 --> 00:44:06.670
But I can't do that.

1048
00:44:06.670 --> 00:44:07.890
I'm not strong enough.

1049
00:44:08.330 --> 00:44:11.070
And I just got so tired of hearing that.

1050
00:44:11.630 --> 00:44:13.270
And I thought, you know what?

1051
00:44:13.370 --> 00:44:18.750
I'm going to talk about the things that I've experienced and some of the hardships that I've had.

1052
00:44:20.450 --> 00:44:24.310
And that feels comfortable for me because it's my life and I've lived it.

1053
00:44:25.670 --> 00:44:27.430
And kind of the rest is history.

1054
00:44:27.590 --> 00:44:32.630
And so I wanted people to know that what I've done in my life, I mean, I'm 65, you guys.

1055
00:44:32.630 --> 00:44:36.230
I did this TED Talk 21 days before I turned 65.

1056
00:44:36.470 --> 00:44:37.210
Unreal to me.

1057
00:44:37.270 --> 00:44:42.690
When I say that, it just feels like I'm talking about somebody else because I feel like I'm 30 years old.

1058
00:44:43.490 --> 00:44:50.350
And there are so many women in their 40s that are younger than all of us that say,

1059
00:44:50.350 --> 00:44:52.750
I can't start something new now.

1060
00:44:53.110 --> 00:44:54.130
Are you insane?

1061
00:44:54.570 --> 00:44:56.970
I mean, I'm going to start something new until I can't breathe.

1062
00:44:57.650 --> 00:45:00.750
You know, we always have an opportunity to do something new.

1063
00:45:00.970 --> 00:45:02.910
I mean, look at the empire you guys are creating.

1064
00:45:04.030 --> 00:45:04.890
Recognize people.

1065
00:45:06.650 --> 00:45:08.070
Recognize Jenna and Angela.

1066
00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:17.010
So it was really an exercise for me to come out of from behind the camera to share my story.

1067
00:45:17.290 --> 00:45:21.590
And everything that I talked about just sort of came out.

1068
00:45:21.790 --> 00:45:26.070
I wrote it, but I also just spoke from my heart.

1069
00:45:36.680 --> 00:45:39.600
Okay, Terry, it is time for the call sheet questions.

1070
00:45:39.960 --> 00:45:40.940
I'm going to go firSt.

1071
00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:41.900
There are five.

1072
00:45:42.160 --> 00:45:42.740
Okay.

1073
00:45:43.020 --> 00:45:46.620
Number one, what was your first entertainment job?

1074
00:45:46.620 --> 00:45:49.280
I was a floater assistant at ICM.

1075
00:45:49.540 --> 00:45:51.300
Oh, what's a floater assistant?

1076
00:45:51.560 --> 00:45:53.960
You do not have a permanent assignment.

1077
00:45:54.320 --> 00:45:59.500
You only, you're called in when an assistant is sick or on vacation.

1078
00:45:59.560 --> 00:46:04.320
And you just literally sit down in a seat and you work for the agent for the day or the week

1079
00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:05.920
or wherever you're needed.

1080
00:46:05.920 --> 00:46:08.420
So you go wherever they tell you to go.

1081
00:46:08.580 --> 00:46:09.760
And it was a temp job.

1082
00:46:10.160 --> 00:46:11.020
Oh, okay.

1083
00:46:11.720 --> 00:46:13.320
All right, I've got the next one.

1084
00:46:13.800 --> 00:46:15.640
Do you speak any other languages?

1085
00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:17.420
Well, un poquito.

1086
00:46:18.560 --> 00:46:24.360
I strive to speak better Spanish because one of my most favorite places on earth is El Salvador,

1087
00:46:25.280 --> 00:46:27.420
which is where I surf a lot.

1088
00:46:27.680 --> 00:46:29.860
So a little Spanish, some Hebrew.

1089
00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:34.100
I think that's probably it.

1090
00:46:35.060 --> 00:46:38.260
Well, this leads very well into the next question.

1091
00:46:38.520 --> 00:46:38.860
It does.

1092
00:46:39.120 --> 00:46:41.360
And maybe you'll expand on this.

1093
00:46:42.080 --> 00:46:45.160
What's a place you've been to that you absolutely loved?

1094
00:46:45.720 --> 00:46:46.140
El Salvador.

1095
00:46:46.420 --> 00:46:46.560
Yeah.

1096
00:46:47.100 --> 00:46:47.400
Yes.

1097
00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:50.680
I've been going there for the past 10 years now.

1098
00:46:50.980 --> 00:46:55.840
My best friend in the world has introduced me to it because that's where I've been going to

1099
00:46:56.740 --> 00:46:59.600
surf camps and also to her wellness retreats.

1100
00:46:59.600 --> 00:47:03.520
And I just fell in love with the country.

1101
00:47:03.700 --> 00:47:05.660
And she's since moved there.

1102
00:47:06.280 --> 00:47:07.840
Pixia Sia, I see you.

1103
00:47:08.080 --> 00:47:11.200
And it was a dream that she had for 10 years.

1104
00:47:11.380 --> 00:47:12.960
And she's an incredible surfer.

1105
00:47:13.060 --> 00:47:14.880
But she's also in the wellness industry.

1106
00:47:15.000 --> 00:47:17.780
And she would take people outside their comfort zone.

1107
00:47:17.780 --> 00:47:20.720
So we've been to Puerto Rico, Nicaragua, El Salvador.

1108
00:47:21.440 --> 00:47:23.640
And that's where I kind of learned.

1109
00:47:23.800 --> 00:47:24.920
That's where I learned how to surf.

1110
00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:26.700
That's where I first cliff dived.

1111
00:47:27.060 --> 00:47:28.860
That's where I did my first ice bath.

1112
00:47:29.100 --> 00:47:31.380
Did all these things that I didn't think I would do in my life.

1113
00:47:31.400 --> 00:47:31.840
I'm sorry.

1114
00:47:31.900 --> 00:47:33.120
What is a cliff dive?

1115
00:47:33.440 --> 00:47:36.280
So you go on a cliff and you jump off into a...

1116
00:47:36.280 --> 00:47:37.580
You just jump off the cliff.

1117
00:47:37.620 --> 00:47:38.340
Yes, you do.

1118
00:47:40.320 --> 00:47:40.720
Yeah.

1119
00:47:41.220 --> 00:47:42.340
And I'm afraid of heights.

1120
00:47:42.640 --> 00:47:43.360
Yeah, I am too.

1121
00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:45.480
And I recently...

1122
00:47:45.480 --> 00:47:46.800
I was there three weeks ago.

1123
00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:49.540
And not only did I jump off the cliff, but I jumped twice.

1124
00:47:50.580 --> 00:47:50.920
Nice.

1125
00:47:51.220 --> 00:47:52.160
I ziplined.

1126
00:47:52.360 --> 00:47:54.040
And I ziplined for the first time.

1127
00:47:54.040 --> 00:47:54.960
And that was terrifying.

1128
00:47:54.960 --> 00:47:55.660
But I did it.

1129
00:47:55.740 --> 00:47:57.280
Because you have to step off that ledge.

1130
00:47:57.280 --> 00:47:58.140
And you're like, whew.

1131
00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:00.940
And then if you kind of do one of those look downs.

1132
00:48:01.140 --> 00:48:03.480
And you realize that you're thousands of feet in the air.

1133
00:48:03.620 --> 00:48:03.760
Yeah.

1134
00:48:04.440 --> 00:48:10.940
Do we need a ladies' trip to a wellness retreat in El Salvador?

1135
00:48:11.340 --> 00:48:12.380
Yes, you do.

1136
00:48:12.380 --> 00:48:14.100
Because it will change your life.

1137
00:48:14.300 --> 00:48:15.360
So you'll send us a link?

1138
00:48:15.540 --> 00:48:16.300
Yes, I will.

1139
00:48:16.440 --> 00:48:19.880
I surfed for the first time, I think, in 2018 on her retreat.

1140
00:48:19.900 --> 00:48:21.240
And I was scared to death.

1141
00:48:21.440 --> 00:48:23.220
And now it's just so liberating.

1142
00:48:23.320 --> 00:48:27.040
I want to point out that 2018 is when you started surfing.

1143
00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:28.600
So you were over the age of 50.

1144
00:48:29.000 --> 00:48:30.060
I was 55.

1145
00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:30.920
Okay.

1146
00:48:31.260 --> 00:48:35.060
I was 56 because I did my first triathlon when I was 55.

1147
00:48:36.120 --> 00:48:36.540
Wow.

1148
00:48:37.260 --> 00:48:37.660
See?

1149
00:48:37.940 --> 00:48:38.980
This is what I'm saying.

1150
00:48:39.020 --> 00:48:41.380
And also, you guys, I did not know how to swim.

1151
00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:42.380
What?

1152
00:48:42.720 --> 00:48:44.540
And I did not know how to swim.

1153
00:48:44.720 --> 00:48:46.900
When you signed up to be in a triathlon?

1154
00:48:47.360 --> 00:48:51.200
So I had to be taught how to swim and then get in that ocean.

1155
00:48:51.460 --> 00:48:51.780
Yeah.

1156
00:48:51.920 --> 00:48:53.040
And swim a mile.

1157
00:48:53.500 --> 00:48:54.160
Wow, Terri.

1158
00:48:54.160 --> 00:48:57.060
So the first triathlon I did was in Kona, Hawaii.

1159
00:48:57.120 --> 00:48:59.740
And it took me an hour to go a mile.

1160
00:48:59.960 --> 00:49:00.800
In the water?

1161
00:49:00.980 --> 00:49:01.560
In the water.

1162
00:49:01.860 --> 00:49:03.560
I mean, it was exhausting.

1163
00:49:03.940 --> 00:49:04.660
Yeah, it is.

1164
00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:10.360
You know, my manager, Naomi, sometimes there'll be like a project or a job opportunity or something.

1165
00:49:10.380 --> 00:49:14.700
And it'll seem very daunting to me for whatever reason, either creatively or logistically.

1166
00:49:14.980 --> 00:49:17.180
But yet there's a reason I want to do it.

1167
00:49:17.180 --> 00:49:22.980
And she'll say, Jenna, you don't have to figure out all the details before you say yes.

1168
00:49:23.220 --> 00:49:27.440
Say yes, and then you will rise to the occasion and be ready when it's time.

1169
00:49:27.620 --> 00:49:29.780
So just say yes, and you'll get yourself there.

1170
00:49:30.080 --> 00:49:31.240
Hello, Naomi.

1171
00:49:32.060 --> 00:49:32.520
Yeah.

1172
00:49:32.520 --> 00:49:35.300
That is the best advice, and she's right.

1173
00:49:35.580 --> 00:49:38.600
You can't—you don't have to have it all figured out.

1174
00:49:38.920 --> 00:49:41.960
Say yes, and then you determine what feels good for you.

1175
00:49:42.500 --> 00:49:43.540
Question number four.

1176
00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:45.260
What do you like to do on the weekends?

1177
00:49:46.380 --> 00:49:46.820
Surf.

1178
00:49:48.460 --> 00:49:49.320
Sensing a theme.

1179
00:49:51.300 --> 00:49:55.020
You know, I'm writing a lot, you guys, because I'm writing a book.

1180
00:49:55.200 --> 00:49:56.780
Yeah, we wanted to ask you about that.

1181
00:49:56.840 --> 00:49:58.460
Can you tell us about your upcoming book?

1182
00:49:58.460 --> 00:50:04.660
Yeah, I think, you know, I started writing a book proposal the day after the writer's

1183
00:50:04.660 --> 00:50:09.140
strike happened, because I'd wanted to write for a long time, and I never had the time to

1184
00:50:09.140 --> 00:50:13.340
do it because I was busy helping everybody else write their things or do their, you know,

1185
00:50:13.600 --> 00:50:14.460
develop their projects.

1186
00:50:14.660 --> 00:50:21.900
And so I just sat down and just started blathering things and wrote a book proposal and got myself

1187
00:50:21.900 --> 00:50:23.060
a literary agent.

1188
00:50:23.480 --> 00:50:28.300
She took it out to some publishers, which is out there right now, and I don't like it

1189
00:50:28.300 --> 00:50:28.740
anymore.

1190
00:50:29.620 --> 00:50:30.720
So because I've grown.

1191
00:50:30.720 --> 00:50:31.820
Wait, you don't like your book proposal?

1192
00:50:31.820 --> 00:50:32.580
I don't like it.

1193
00:50:32.580 --> 00:50:33.860
Is it fiction or nonfiction?

1194
00:50:34.000 --> 00:50:34.520
It's my life.

1195
00:50:34.640 --> 00:50:35.360
It's nonfiction.

1196
00:50:35.660 --> 00:50:36.320
Oh, memoir.

1197
00:50:36.600 --> 00:50:39.760
It's memoir, but it also has practical guides in it.

1198
00:50:40.220 --> 00:50:41.940
Here's how I navigated this thing.

1199
00:50:42.140 --> 00:50:42.460
Yeah, yeah.

1200
00:50:42.840 --> 00:50:44.440
Here's how you can step into your power.

1201
00:50:44.860 --> 00:50:45.940
So what now?

1202
00:50:46.140 --> 00:50:47.920
It's out in the world at publishers.

1203
00:50:47.920 --> 00:50:48.780
I'm going to pull it back.

1204
00:50:48.920 --> 00:50:49.640
You're going to pull it back.

1205
00:50:49.640 --> 00:50:53.680
I'm going to have the courage to say, thank you.

1206
00:50:53.680 --> 00:50:57.900
If you have read this or if it's sitting on your desk, please take it off.

1207
00:50:58.020 --> 00:51:02.240
Because I have grown so much, even in the last two years.

1208
00:51:02.600 --> 00:51:04.540
My voice is different.

1209
00:51:05.340 --> 00:51:07.820
What I want to talk about is different.

1210
00:51:08.140 --> 00:51:10.860
And so I'm just rewriting it from page one.

1211
00:51:11.020 --> 00:51:12.380
My God, I love you, Terri.

1212
00:51:12.540 --> 00:51:13.860
Terri, this is what I love about you.

1213
00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:15.080
I love you.

1214
00:51:15.260 --> 00:51:20.240
It's kind of what you said before about how like, if you put a project out there and then

1215
00:51:20.240 --> 00:51:22.980
it doesn't end up feeling right, don't be afraid.

1216
00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:24.420
To just, yeah.

1217
00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:24.920
Yeah.

1218
00:51:25.220 --> 00:51:26.000
Because we grow.

1219
00:51:26.180 --> 00:51:27.140
We change so much.

1220
00:51:27.380 --> 00:51:27.460
Yeah.

1221
00:51:27.860 --> 00:51:32.480
You know, and I figured if I'm going to put myself out into the world and if there's a

1222
00:51:32.480 --> 00:51:37.880
teaching component to the book, I've done so many more speaking kinds of things.

1223
00:51:37.880 --> 00:51:43.580
And I've been exposed to so many more women and people in different industries.

1224
00:51:43.860 --> 00:51:46.600
That is that I don't just talk about entertainment.

1225
00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:47.520
Yeah.

1226
00:51:47.520 --> 00:51:48.940
You know, I can talk to C-suite women.

1227
00:51:49.160 --> 00:51:52.760
I did a huge conversation with women who are on Capitol Hill.

1228
00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:56.480
You know, I'm not in politics, but the connection was storytelling.

1229
00:51:57.020 --> 00:51:57.160
Yeah.

1230
00:51:57.420 --> 00:52:02.880
You know, so there's so many things that I can do and talk about now that I want to put

1231
00:52:02.880 --> 00:52:03.200
in there.

1232
00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:05.520
So I just said, f*** it, I'm just going to write the book.

1233
00:52:05.880 --> 00:52:06.020
Yeah.

1234
00:52:06.100 --> 00:52:06.800
Not write a proposal.

1235
00:52:06.940 --> 00:52:07.700
I'm just going to write a book.

1236
00:52:07.720 --> 00:52:08.820
I'll put the book out there.

1237
00:52:08.820 --> 00:52:10.920
And if I don't get a publisher, I'll self-publish.

1238
00:52:10.920 --> 00:52:11.780
Self-publish.

1239
00:52:12.120 --> 00:52:13.980
That's what I did with my acting book, Terry.

1240
00:52:14.600 --> 00:52:14.920
You did?

1241
00:52:14.920 --> 00:52:16.900
I wrote the book, An Actor's Life.

1242
00:52:17.120 --> 00:52:22.080
And it's a combination of memoir and then also advice for aspiring actors.

1243
00:52:22.340 --> 00:52:22.960
Take it or leave it.

1244
00:52:23.060 --> 00:52:24.080
You know, it's just my story.

1245
00:52:24.200 --> 00:52:25.180
Everybody has a different one.

1246
00:52:25.340 --> 00:52:25.660
That's right.

1247
00:52:25.660 --> 00:52:32.520
But I ended up writing it, and it got published by a small publishing house, a family-run business

1248
00:52:32.520 --> 00:52:33.060
in Texas.

1249
00:52:34.040 --> 00:52:36.600
But I wrote it on spec, as they say.

1250
00:52:36.820 --> 00:52:39.180
You know, I didn't have an advance, but I wrote it from the heart.

1251
00:52:39.520 --> 00:52:39.700
Yeah.

1252
00:52:39.820 --> 00:52:45.500
And what I liked about that is I had also taken out a proposal to big publishers, and they

1253
00:52:45.500 --> 00:52:46.420
wanted me to tweak it.

1254
00:52:46.480 --> 00:52:49.440
They said, well, this is great, but can you also talk about what it's like to be a mom

1255
00:52:49.440 --> 00:52:50.000
in Hollywood?

1256
00:52:50.380 --> 00:52:53.780
Can you also talk about stories from the office?

1257
00:52:54.020 --> 00:52:55.060
Can you tell us about that?

1258
00:52:55.060 --> 00:52:57.780
Can you tell us about, like—and I said, that's not what this book is.

1259
00:52:57.840 --> 00:52:58.700
That's a great book.

1260
00:52:58.700 --> 00:52:59.740
That sounds wonderful.

1261
00:52:59.920 --> 00:53:01.900
That's not the book I'm looking to write.

1262
00:53:02.060 --> 00:53:05.200
So I just wrote it myself, and it was great.

1263
00:53:05.420 --> 00:53:10.620
That's full circle to you can't be—don't be afraid to, if you're putting a project out

1264
00:53:10.620 --> 00:53:16.740
there, you know, to have somebody say, oh, we love this, but we would like you to make

1265
00:53:16.740 --> 00:53:22.520
these changes to it, or you must cast this certain person or do those things within our

1266
00:53:22.520 --> 00:53:24.520
industry that don't feel good.

1267
00:53:25.060 --> 00:53:29.820
And you're at a point in your life where you can say no and have the confidence to say

1268
00:53:29.820 --> 00:53:31.360
no and feel good about it.

1269
00:53:31.500 --> 00:53:38.620
And also, you're recognizing that you're still, even at 65, learning who you are, what your

1270
00:53:38.620 --> 00:53:40.320
voice is, and what you want to share.

1271
00:53:40.680 --> 00:53:42.200
You're still having discovery.

1272
00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:44.280
So that's great.

